# Making a container run's tree checksum attributable, 2026-10-04

`scripts/verify-in-docker.sh` checksums the source tree before the first gate and again
after the last one, so a run that overlapped an edit can be dismissed instead of believed.
For two days the attribution was one bare number, and a number quoted out of a transcript
could not be re-measured: `TODO.md` carried an open row saying a `--full` run's checksum did
not reproduce from the checkout it was said to describe, and that the listing behind it had
been deleted by the script's own `EXIT` trap. This measures what that line could and could
not say, fixes the parts that were actually broken, and records which part is still unknown.

== 0. The row being decided

The row, written on 2026-10-04 and left open by the batch that put the directory and the
commit on the line:

    == source tree: 4020 files, checksum 2692478763

measured today in the same launch directory, still at `36d0809e` with
`git status --porcelain` empty: 4020 paths and `3045864519`; a fresh `file://` clone of it
also `3045864519`. The inference written next to it was that the path count agrees, so the
difference must be in file contents. That inference is unsound, and section 3 measures why.
The two claims worth settling first were whether the recipe reproduces at all, and whether
the instrument reads the same tree in every place it can be run.

== 1. Two other bare checksums reproduce exactly, from clean clones of the commits they name

The only other runs whose fingerprint line survives in a committed log are two `--full`
sweeps:

    $ grep -n 'source tree:' docs/verification/verify-in-docker-full-8a52ff0a-2026-10-04.log \
          docs/verification/verify-in-docker-full-a04e91a7-2026-10-04.log

    docs/verification/verify-in-docker-full-8a52ff0a-2026-10-04.log:38:    == source tree: 4001 files, checksum 1801880867
    docs/verification/verify-in-docker-full-a04e91a7-2026-10-04.log:41:    == source tree: 3996 files, checksum 4160701699

Each was re-measured by cloning the repository again, detaching at the commit that log
names, and running **that clone's own copy** of the entry point, with `IMAGE_TAG` pointed at
a throwaway tag so `chaos-verify:local` stayed as it was. The line prints before any gate,
so `timeout` is enough to stop the run once the number is in hand:

    $ for s in 8a52ff0a a04e91a7; do
    >   d=/var/tmp/fp-repro-$s
    >   git clone -q --no-hardlinks file:///home/chaos/hunt/dev/projects/chaos-code "$d"
    >   ( cd "$d" && git checkout -q --detach "$s" && git status --porcelain | head -3 \
    >       && echo "clone at $(git rev-parse --short HEAD) clean" )
    > done

    clone at 8a52ff0a clean
    clone at a04e91a7 clean

    $ cd /var/tmp
    $ for s in 8a52ff0a a04e91a7; do
    >   echo "##### clone $s"
    >   IMAGE_TAG=chaos-verify:fp-$s timeout 240 \
    >     bash /var/tmp/fp-repro-$s/scripts/verify-in-docker.sh > /tmp/fp-repro-$s.log 2>&1
    >   echo "exit=$?"
    >   grep -E '^== source tree:|differ from HEAD' /tmp/fp-repro-$s.log
    > done

    ##### clone 8a52ff0a
    exit=124
    == source tree: 4001 files, checksum 1801880867
    ##### clone a04e91a7
    exit=124
    == source tree: 3996 files, checksum 4160701699

`exit=124` is the `timeout` firing while the quick gates were still running; the fingerprint
had already printed. Both numbers came back the same to the digit. The path count is a
property of the commit, which is why the count is what identified the tree in the first
place:

    $ for s in a04e91a7 8a52ff0a 36d0809e c8104865 6826fce0 99ae48fe; do
    >   printf '%s %s\n' "$s" "$(git ls-tree -r --name-only "$s" | wc -l)"
    > done

    a04e91a7 3996
    8a52ff0a 4001
    36d0809e 4020
    c8104865 4027
    6826fce0 4028
    99ae48fe 4031

So the recipe is reproducible: given a checkout with nothing untracked and nothing modified,
the same commit yields the same checksum on any machine, in any container, on any day. A
checksum that does not reproduce is therefore not a broken instrument; it is a true
measurement of a different tree.

The clone ran the entry point as it existed at `8a52ff0a`, where the fingerprint was one
pipeline:

    ( cd "${repo_root}" && git ls-files -co --exclude-standard -z | xargs -0 cksum ) >"$1"

and the split-and-check rewrite that replaced it writes the same bytes:

    $ cd /var/tmp/fp-attrib
    $ (git ls-files -co --exclude-standard -z | xargs -0 cksum) > /tmp/old-recipe.sums
    $ git ls-files -co --exclude-standard -z > /tmp/new-recipe.list
    $ xargs -0 cksum < /tmp/new-recipe.list > /tmp/new-recipe.sums
    $ cmp /tmp/old-recipe.sums /tmp/new-recipe.sums && echo "identical bytes"

    identical bytes
    old=3045864519 new=3045864519

== 2. Same commit, three instruments, three identical numbers

Everything that could plausibly have made the host and the container disagree was measured
on one clean clone at `36d0809e`: the git version, the uid, `HOME`, and whether a global
ignore file is configured.

    $ cd /var/tmp/fp-attrib
    $ git ls-files -co --exclude-standard -z > /tmp/fp-host.list
    $ xargs -0 cksum < /tmp/fp-host.list > /tmp/fp-host.sums
    $ printf 'paths=%s content=%s pathset=%s\n' \
          "$(tr -dc '\0' < /tmp/fp-host.list | wc -c | tr -d ' ')" \
          "$(cksum < /tmp/fp-host.sums | cut -d' ' -f1)" \
          "$(LC_ALL=C sort -z < /tmp/fp-host.list | cksum | cut -d' ' -f1)"

    36d0809e
    --- host (git 2.34.1, uid 1003, HOME=/home/chaos):
    paths=4020
    content=3045864519
    pathset=629663397

    $ docker run --rm --init --workdir /src --volume /var/tmp/fp-attrib:/src \
          -e GIT_CONFIG_COUNT=1 -e GIT_CONFIG_KEY_0=safe.directory -e GIT_CONFIG_VALUE_0=/src \
          chaos-verify:local bash -c 'git ls-files -co --exclude-standard -z > /tmp/c.list;
          xargs -0 cksum < /tmp/c.list > /tmp/c.sums; printf "container (git %s, uid %s, HOME=%s,
          excludes=%s)\n" "$(git --version | cut -d " " -f3)" "$(id -u)" "$HOME"
          "$(git config --global core.excludesFile || echo none)"; printf "paths=%s\ncontent=%s
          \npathset=%s\n" "$(tr -dc "\0" < /tmp/c.list | wc -c | tr -d " ")" \
          "$(cksum < /tmp/c.sums | cut -d " " -f1)" \
          "$(LC_ALL=C sort -z < /tmp/c.list | cksum | cut -d " " -f1)"'

    container (git 2.39.5, uid 0, HOME=/root, excludes=none)
    paths=4020
    content=3045864519
    pathset=629663397

The container's git is five minor versions ahead, runs as root, has a different `HOME` and
no global ignore file, and reports the same three numbers as the host does. `git ls-files`
also emits its listing already in `LC_ALL=C` byte order, so sorting it changes nothing on
this tree; the sort stays because it is what makes the second number a statement about set
membership rather than about order, and section 6 pins that with a listing fed in reverse.

== 3. The file count proved nothing: three shapes, all measured

`cksum` writes `crc bytes path` per file, and the reported checksum is a `cksum` over that
listing. A path therefore contributes to the number twice: once by being present, once by
what it holds. That makes the count and the checksum independent questions, which three
states of one clone show.

Shape A, a tracked file gains a line, no path appears or disappears:

    == clean clone at 36d0809e
    paths=4020 content=3045864519 pathset=629663397

    == A. a tracked file gains a line: no path appears or disappears
    paths=4020 content=3341663371 pathset=629663397

Shape B, a file moved without telling git, which is what a developer's `mv` looks like:

    == B. one path traded for another (a plain mv, git not told about it)
    cksum: scripts/ci/check-guard-wiring.py: No such file or directory
    paths=4021 content=1453745329 pathset=1358900702

The moved-away path is still tracked, so `git ls-files` still lists it and `cksum` still
cannot read it. That is also why the entry point refuses to run gates on such a tree rather
than summing what it can reach: the count went up, not down, because the listing carries the
ghost and the new name both. The same shape inside the fixture repository, produced by
`test_an_unreadable_path_in_the_listing_is_named_rather_than_left_as_a_count`, is refused
with the remedy on its own line:

    $ python3 scripts/ci/test-verify-in-docker.py \
          AttributionTests.test_an_unreadable_path_in_the_listing_is_named_rather_than_left_as_a_count

    cksum: docs/gate-notes.md: No such file or directory
    fingerprint: cksum summed only 4 of 5 listed file(s) from /tmp/tmp1qsohg46/repo (exit 123)
       a partial fingerprint is compared against the run's own after-image and agrees
       with it, so the verdict would name a commit this run did not actually read.
       a tracked path deleted in the worktree is still listed by git and still cannot be
       read: commit the deletion or restore the file, then run this again.

The stub `docker` that the fixtures install records every invocation, and that case asserts
none was made: the refusal happens before any gate, so the run cannot be quoted as a pass.

Shape C, one path traded for another in a way that leaves the count exactly as it was:

    == C. two untracked files traded for each other: same count, different set
    paths=4021 content=3801244303 pathset=2997202510
    paths=4021 content=3940064607 pathset=1581763344

Same number of files, entirely different set, and nothing in the old line could tell this
apart from shape A. That is the hole the row fell into: "the count agrees, so only contents
can differ" is false, and the two numbers now printed side by side are what makes the two
answers separable.

== 4. A regression found while measuring: a run launched from another directory

Splitting the fingerprint into two checked halves moved `git ls-files` inside a
`cd "${repo_root}"` subshell and left the `cksum` half in whatever directory the script had
been launched from. The listing holds paths relative to the repository, so from anywhere else
every one of them is unreadable, and the run stops before its first gate. This is that run,
against the fixture repository, captured while the bug was still in the tree:

    $ python3 scripts/ci/test-verify-in-docker.py \
          AttributionTests.test_the_tree_is_fingerprinted_when_the_script_is_launched_from_elsewhere

    == building stub-image:tag from docker.io/library/debian:bookworm-slim
    sha256:stubbed
    == preparing cargo cache volumes
    cksum: .gitignore: No such file or directory
    cksum: README.md: No such file or directory
    cksum: docker/verify.Dockerfile: No such file or directory
    cksum: docs/gate-notes.md: No such file or directory
    cksum: scripts/verify-in-docker.sh: No such file or directory
    fingerprint: cksum summed only 0 of 5 listed file(s) from /tmp/tmp4hs3gnw_/repo (exit 123)
       a partial fingerprint is compared against the run's own after-image and agrees
       with it, so the verdict would name a commit this run did not actually read.

The script derives `repo_root` from `$0` precisely so it can be run by absolute path, and
the fixtures set `cwd` to the repository in every case, so nothing caught it. The `cd` is
now part of the `cksum` half, and one fixture runs the shipped script from a directory that
is not the repository and compares its line against the same tree measured from the
repository root.

== 5. What the line says now, from a run against a real container

    $ CHAOS_TREE_MANIFEST=/var/tmp/fp-manifest2 scripts/verify-in-docker.sh \
          --only "container runner fixtures"

    == building chaos-verify:local from docker.io/library/debian:bookworm-slim
    == preparing cargo cache volumes
    == source tree: /home/chaos/hunt/dev/projects/chaos-code at 99ae48fe: 4031 files, checksum 1155391559 (path set 2935949943)
       tree listings kept in /var/tmp/fp-manifest2: before.list, before.sums
       2 path(s) differ from HEAD, so this run describes the working tree, not a commit
    (… 58 lines elided here)
    selected gates passed in chaos-verify:local
      --only was in effect: 1 of 37 gates ran, so this is not a full sweep

The two numbers separate the two questions, and the kept listing is what makes a later
reader able to check the claim instead of re-running it. Two independent re-measurements of
that same tree agree with the line:

    $ git ls-files -co --exclude-standard -z > /tmp/g1.list
    $ xargs -0 cksum < /tmp/g1.list > /tmp/g1.sums
    $ cmp /var/tmp/fp-manifest2/before.sums /tmp/g1.sums && echo "byte-identical"

    byte-identical
    paths=4031
    content=1155391559
    pathset=2935949943

and a second run of the same untouched tree writes a listing no gate could tell apart from
the first:

    $ diff /var/tmp/fp-manifest/before.sums /var/tmp/fp-manifest2/after.sums && echo "identical"

    identical

When the two images do disagree, the report says which half moved instead of leaving the
reader to diff it. Both blocks below are the tail of the stdout the two classification
fixtures assert on, captured from a run of them:

    $ python3 scripts/ci/test-verify-in-docker.py \
          AttributionTests.test_a_content_move_mid_run_is_called_a_content_move

    UNATTRIBUTABLE: the source tree changed while the gates ran.
      checksum 2292906840 -> 2278288267; path set 3123974866 unchanged, so file contents moved:
        docs/gate-notes.md
      nothing in this run can be attributed to a commit; re-run it with the tree at rest.
      both listings are in /tmp/tmpf20q_2un/kept: diff before.sums after.sums

or, when a path appeared while the gates ran:

    $ python3 scripts/ci/test-verify-in-docker.py \
          AttributionTests.test_a_path_appearing_mid_run_is_called_a_path_set_move

    UNATTRIBUTABLE: the source tree changed while the gates ran.
      checksum 2292906840 -> 166729144; path set moved (3123974866 -> 360617741), so a path appeared, vanished or was renamed:
        docs/new-mid-run.md
      nothing in this run can be attributed to a commit; re-run it with the tree at rest.

`CHAOS_TREE_MANIFEST` names the directory the four files go in (`before.list`,
`before.sums`, `after.list`, `after.sums`). A directory that cannot be created stops the run
with exit 2 before an image is built: a run asked to keep evidence and unable to keep it
must not go on to print a verdict as though it had. Without the variable nothing is written
anywhere and the listings still die in a temporary directory, which is the behaviour every
existing transcript describes.

== 6. Fixtures and the mutation matrix

`scripts/ci/test-verify-in-docker.py` drives the shipped script with a stub `docker` and a
throwaway repository. It went from 34 to 46 cases; 12 were added, and every one of them is
about the shape of a number rather than its value:

    test_the_line_carries_a_path_set_number_next_to_the_checksum
    test_an_untouched_tree_agrees_with_itself_on_both_numbers
    test_editing_a_files_bytes_moves_the_checksum_and_leaves_the_path_set_alone
    test_swapping_one_path_for_another_keeps_the_count_and_moves_the_path_set
    test_the_path_set_number_ignores_the_order_git_named_the_paths_in
    test_the_tree_is_fingerprinted_when_the_script_is_launched_from_elsewhere
    test_an_unreadable_path_in_the_listing_is_named_rather_than_left_as_a_count
    test_a_content_move_mid_run_is_called_a_content_move
    test_a_path_appearing_mid_run_is_called_a_path_set_move
    test_the_manifest_keeps_both_listings_and_they_show_the_move
    test_a_run_at_rest_keeps_two_listings_that_are_identical
    test_a_manifest_directory_that_cannot_be_made_builds_no_image

The order case is the one that could not be produced by setting anything up: git will not be
persuaded to list a tree out of order, so a `git` earlier on `PATH` reverses the listing it
got from the real one and delegates everything else. That single fixture is what proves the
content checksum is order-sensitive and the path-set digest is not.

Each mutation was applied to `scripts/verify-in-docker.sh`, the whole file run against it,
and the file restored with `cp` and checked with `cmp` afterwards (`restored byte-identical`
at the end of the run). The driver refuses to start from a non-green baseline; the baseline
was 46 green.

    baseline: 46 fixtures green

    M01 cksum reads the listing from the caller's directory again        killed  ...launched_from_elsewhere
    M02 the attribution line loses the path-set number                  killed  35 cases, all of them
    M03 the path-set digest stops sorting the listing                   killed  ...ignores_the_order_...
    M04 keep_manifest never keeps anything                              killed  2 manifest cases
    M05 an unusable manifest directory no longer stops the run           killed  ...builds_no_image
    M06 the classification of a move is inverted                        killed  both classification cases
    M07 the UNATTRIBUTABLE report stops pointing at the kept listings    killed  ...show_the_move
    M08 the after-image is never copied out                              killed  2 manifest cases
    M09 the unreadable-path message loses its remedy                    killed  ...named_rather_than_left_as_a_count
    M10 the path-set field prints the file count instead of a digest     killed  ...swapping_one_path..., ...show_the_move
    M11 paths_of returns a constant                                     killed  3 cases

    11/11 killed

M02 is reported honestly as a weak kill: deleting that line leaves the preceding `echo`
argument hanging on a backslash continuation, so the script stops parsing and every case
that runs it fails. M10 and M11 were added as the semantic versions of the same question,
and they are killed by the specific cases that care about the field.

== 7. Reproduce it

    $ python3 scripts/ci/test-verify-in-docker.py            # 46 cases, ~5 s
    $ scripts/verify-in-docker.sh --only "container runner fixtures"

To see the two numbers disagree the way the row could not, in a throwaway clone of this
repository:

    $ git clone -q --no-hardlinks "$PWD" /tmp/fp-demo && cd /tmp/fp-demo
    $ measure() { git ls-files -co --exclude-standard -z > /tmp/f.list;
    >   xargs -0 cksum < /tmp/f.list > /tmp/f.sums;
    >   printf 'paths=%s content=%s pathset=%s\n' \
    >     "$(tr -dc '\0' < /tmp/f.list | wc -c | tr -d ' ')" \
    >     "$(cksum < /tmp/f.sums | cut -d ' ' -f1)" \
    >     "$(LC_ALL=C sort -z < /tmp/f.list | cksum | cut -d ' ' -f1)"; }
    $ measure
    $ echo first > zz-a.md; measure; rm zz-a.md
    $ echo second > zz-b.md; measure; rm zz-b.md

The last two lines are shape C: the same `paths=` both times, and both digests moved.

To check the reproducibility claim behind section 1, clone the repository at any commit in
the history, run the entry point there with `--only` naming one cheap gate, and compare its
line against a `--full` transcript of the same commit.

== 8. What is still not known

The specific number the row quotes, `2692478763`, is still not identified. Its transcript
was never retained and the checkout it was measured in was deleted during cleanup, so there
is nothing left to diff, and section 1 has shown only that a checksum of that kind is a true
measurement of *some* tree. The count of 4020 is consistent with a clean `36d0809e` and with
at least two other trees on this machine that day: a checkout at `0faeee1d`, whose tracked
count is 4018, with two untracked paths in it would print 4020 as well, and shape A and
shape C above both move the checksum without contradicting the count.

What changed is that this can no longer happen invisibly. A line that cannot be re-measured
is now a line about a named directory at a named commit, with a digest of the path set
beside the digest of the bytes and, when `CHAOS_TREE_MANIFEST` is set, the per-path listing
kept on disk afterwards.
